Longest absolute file path Strings & arrays  A text representation lists filesystem entries on separate lines, with leading tabs indicating depth. Find the greatest full file-path length, including slash separators; names containing a dot are files, and a representation with no files has length zero.   Official problem and judge on LeetCode ↗    Constraints   Is the input a string?   Yes   Can we assume the input is valid?   No   Will there always be a file in the input?   Yes   Is the output an int?   Yes   Can we assume this fits memory?   Yes    Test Cases  root/newline-tab-src/newline-tab-tab-a.js represents root/src/a.js, length 13  Also check empty inputs and boundary sizes. The JavaScript companion includes runnable assertions.  Algorithm  We'll use a dictionary path_len to keep track of the current character length (values) at each depth (keys). Depth 0 will have a length of 0.  Initialize max_len to 0.  Split the input based on the newline character. Iterate on each resulting line:   Extract the name by excluding the tab characters  Calculate the depth length by taking into account the tab characters  If we are dealing with a file path (there is a '.' in name)   Calculate path_len[depth] + len(name) and update max_len if needed   Else, update path_len[depth + 1] = path_len[depth] + len(name) + 1   We add + 1 because each depth is separated by the '/' character   Return max_len   Complexity:   Time: O(n)  Space: O(n)   Code   class Solution(object):

    def length_longest_path(self, file_system):
        if not isinstance(file_system, str):
            raise TypeError('file_system must be a string')
        max_len = 0
        path_len = {0: 0}
        for line in file_system.splitlines():
            name = line.lstrip('\t')
            depth = len(line) - len(name)
            if '.' in name:
                max_len = max(max_len, path_len[depth] + len(name))
            else:
                path_len[depth + 1] = path_len[depth] + len(name) + 1
        return max_len

